original.detail:
Thank you: review confirmed the state definitions, precedence, trusted-body parsing, dedupe across polls and restarts (state and feed item in one transaction), the more-than-50 case, feed compatibility and redaction.

One defect, reproduced with fake responses: children are resolved against the parent repository's issues by number (~114), and blockers are matched the same way (~132). GitHub allows sub-issues from other repositories, so:
(a) an unapproved `other/repo#3` child plus an unrelated, prioritized `owner/repo#3` suppresses `needs_approval`;
(b) a closed sibling `other/repo#2` and an open child blocked by an unrelated `owner/repo#2` emit `ready_for_acceptance`.
Fix: request `repository { nameWithOwner }` (or node ids) for sub-issues and blockers in the query (~220), and key every child, record and blocker match by repository plus number. A child in another repository whose state or labels the queue doesn't read counts as not approved.

Add these tests from review:

```ts
test.each(["approval", "native acceptance"] as const)(
  "epic attention does not substitute a local issue for a foreign child during %s",
  async (kind) => {
    const h = harness();
    const { epic, open, closed } = attentionEpic();
    epic.body = "";
    const foreign = {
      ...(kind === "approval" ? open : closed),
      repository: { nameWithOwner: "other/repo" },
      labels: { totalCount: 0, nodes: [] },
    };
    epic.subIssues.nodes = kind === "approval" ? [closed, foreign] : [foreign, open];
    if (kind === "native acceptance") {
      // This blocker is owner/repo#2, whereas the closed sibling is other/repo#2.
      const localBlocker = {
        number: 2,
        state: "CLOSED" as const,
        repository: { nameWithOwner: "owner/repo" },
      };
      open.blockedBy = { totalCount: 1, nodes: [localBlocker] };
    }
    h.data.set("owner/repo", [epic, open]);
    h.start();
    await h.advance();
    expect(
      h.store
        .readFeed()
        .items.filter((item) => item.kind === "queue.epic_attention")
        .map((item) => item.data.state),
    ).toEqual(kind === "approval" ? ["needs_approval"] : []);
  },
);
```

Also: the feed-CLI compatibility test passes on main too (generic rendering), so it doesn't show the change. Keep it as a compatibility check, but make sure another test fails without the change for that path.

