original.detail:
The slice matches #953 closely: 248 source lines, all 15 numbered test groups present and mutation-sensitive, authenticated decryption before any plaintext, fixed diagnostics, no retries on writes. One spec gap.

`get` checks cancellation only after envelope authentication and digest/size validation (src/integrations/shared-store.ts:243). If the caller aborts after the body has been fully read and the frame is damaged, `open()` throws `corrupt` (or `unsupported` for an unknown version) before `get` can return `aborted`. That violates #953's cancellation precedence and reports an interrupted read as damaged storage.

Fix: check the caller's signal immediately after the blob read completes, before parsing or validating the envelope, and reject `aborted`. Keep the existing order everywhere else.

Add this regression in `describe("cancellation and deadlines")`. It fails today with `corrupt` and passes with the fix:

```ts
  test("cancellation: get abort takes precedence over validation after its complete body", async () => {
    const remote = new Remote();
    const payload = bytesOf("cancelled read");
    const { blob } = await remote.client().put(runId, role, keyA, payload);
    const sha = remote.retained(runId).get(keyA) ?? "";
    const frame = Buffer.from(remote.blobs.get(sha) ?? "", "base64");
    frame[frame.length - 1] = (frame[frame.length - 1] ?? 0) ^ 1;
    remote.blobs.set(sha, frame.toString("base64"));
    const controller = new AbortController();
    let readingBlob = false;
    intercept(remote, isRead("git/blobs/"), async (_call, next) => {
      readingBlob = true;
      return next();
    });
    const client = remote.client({
      deadline: () => ({
        signal: new AbortController().signal,
        // Disposal marks complete body consumption, before get validates the envelope.
        dispose: () => readingBlob && controller.abort(),
      }),
    });
    await rejects(client.get(runId, role, blob, controller.signal), "aborted");
  });

```

